00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:25.200
Now let's zoom in to specific
control features, like IAM.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.260
In this year alone, we
actually enhanced IAM

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.650
so much that there
is a specific talk,

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.770
actually, later today
and tomorrow about IAM

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.330
best practices.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:42.059
Let me give a small glimpse
about what IAM has to offer.

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:43.799
The new thing we
did this year was we

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:46.380
talked through how we
can organize resources

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.134
in such a way that
we want to think

00:08:48.134 --> 00:08:49.300
big for the big enterprises.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:53.059
So let's say that you have
a big, huge organizations

00:08:53.059 --> 00:08:54.600
and then all these
sort of Home Depot

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.099
and all these
enterprises coming in.

00:08:56.099 --> 00:08:58.410
We wanted to create a node
called Organization node.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.560
Inside the Organization node,
you can divide, let's say,

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.670
engineering department, finance
department, and HR department.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:07.850
All of these are different
folders you can create.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:11.130
Folders are a very
flexible way to organize.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.500
You can organize your
enterprises differently.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.730
Let's say, you can
organize them by partners

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:17.955
or the companies you bought.

00:09:17.955 --> 00:09:20.220
Let's say, a company
buys another company.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.440
You can bring that as
another folder there.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.260
And then within the
folder, you have Projects.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.740
Projects sees the entity
that we had for a long time.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.590
All the billing, all
the quota enforcement,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:34.780
all kinds of attribution has to
happen in the Projects level.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:36.550
So usually, Project
can be, let's say,

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:38.970
divided as test project
or development project.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.160
Or you can say, Project
can be microservices.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.340
For example, Spotify gives
each microservice a project.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:46.890
Between the project,
you can bring up

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:49.710
any Resources you want,
either networking resources,

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.670
compute resources,
or storage resources,

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:52.920
as you can see in the diagram.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.190
Now, coming to the
policies, what are the IAM

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:00.080
features that we offer?

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.440
Just let me give a simple,
brief description about IAM,

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.150
how we think about IAM.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.601
There's just who.

00:10:08.601 --> 00:10:09.100
Who is IAM?

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:12.520
Who here means, actually,
the identities and roles.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:13.544
And what?

00:10:13.544 --> 00:10:14.710
What are the tools you have?

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.810
What are the policies
that can apply to where,

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:21.740
to where are the resources
that we just saw?

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:24.800
In other words, Google
offers two main things

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:26.030
for the identities and roles.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:29.670
If you go look inside
our [INAUDIBLE] console,

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:33.110
you will see you can use
Google Groups as identities.

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.455
You can use your email
address as identities.

00:10:35.455 --> 00:10:37.580
And we introduced something
called Service Account.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:40.610
Service accounts is-- let's say,
you downloaded a third party

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:41.430
application.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.430
You don't want to trust
that, because maybe it's

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.522
hundreds and thousands
of lines of code there.

00:10:45.522 --> 00:10:47.480
You don't know what that
particular application

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:48.500
is doing.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.620
You can attach an identity to
that particular third party

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:52.520
code.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:55.440
That's what a
service account is.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:58.330
And IAM policies are the verbs.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:00.530
You can think of actions.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.100
For example, you can say,
at this particular instance,

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.930
admin can have a view
permission, list permission,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:09.760
add/delete permissions.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.000
And then you can actually
add some kind of custom verbs

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:14.020
to it.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:16.320
We do have custom
verbs, like setBacks,

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.090
setLabels are all custom verbs.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.670
And then we have Resources
that we just saw,

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.060
like big hierarchies
of resources.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.280
So all of these are possible.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.180
You can mix and match
the roles and identities

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.920
and the policies of the verbs.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.040
And then the resources,
you can mix and match.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.802
It's because of big metrics
of clear demarcation

00:11:35.802 --> 00:11:37.510
and authentication
you have to play with,

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.810
like who should get what
permissions in each hierarchy.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.430
And it's very clear,
easy to remember

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:45.970
that the hierarchy,
if you go back,

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.610
the hierarchy is,
in the reference,

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.260
you can see that
it's inheritance.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:53.635
The top most-- let's say, if
you give a instance admin,

00:11:53.635 --> 00:11:56.590
like the org admin,
a ownership role,

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.110
he has complete control of
all the resources and all

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:00.370
the projects.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:06.820
So there are best practices
we should actually give when

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.440
you're using IAM at Google.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:12.190
Number one is grant rules
using Google Groups.

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:14.830
Don't use individual
email addresses.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.340
Create a group, and then give
that particular identity,

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.490
and then give a particular
role for that identity.

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.710
Similarly, I think you
should go with something

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:26.470
like a used all-concept
primitives that will already

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:28.900
offer all kinds
of verbs, and try

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:33.220
to give the least common
privileges to the resources.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:34.990
Don't give a very
broad privileges,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:36.910
then it's very difficult
what's going on,

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:40.780
as what happened in the artwork.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.340
It's a security
breach by identity.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.192
They came with cops' identity.

00:12:44.192 --> 00:12:45.150
And then they stole it.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.530
And that they removed the
firewalls, that kind of thing

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:48.260
they did.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:49.510
So the same thing will happen.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:53.950
IAM is a classic dot I want to
connect back to the painting

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.244
that I just showed you.

00:12:55.244 --> 00:12:57.160
Because of the security
breach, it most likely

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:01.700
will happen, because of
the wrong IAM permissions.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:02.950
And then you can go over that.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:07.210
And then tomorrow at 11:20,
you have a great talk

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:09.940
about best practices in IAM.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:11.795
And we are actually
offering more features.

00:13:11.795 --> 00:13:13.270
They'll talk about the feature.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.300
One such feature is
custom IAM roles.

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:19.570
Now, IAM is done.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.630
Let's move on to, let's say,
the perimeter security and VPC

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.390
in deep.

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.570
I don't need to repeat this.

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.500
But traditionally,
the data center,

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.200
if you go to any
third parties, they

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.722
are going to sell you
boxes and proxies.

00:13:33.722 --> 00:13:35.930
And you have to put these
boxes in the middle layers,

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:38.480
like load balancers
and firewalls.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.120
Whenever you need
to grow, you have

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.300
to grow them scale out way, but
performance is going to suffer.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.520
You need to actually
spend a lot of capex

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.680
to buy more of these firewalls,
more of these load balancers,

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:50.500
right?

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:52.200
It's a given.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.590
That's what, actually, all
the enterprises get money

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:55.090
from that.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.070
But they become choke points.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:13:59.380
Let me tell you a
less known secret

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:03.910
about this, why this is going
to fail in the very short-term.

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:08.020
The networking bandwidth
is increasing very rapidly.

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:11.920
We just saw 1 gig to 10
gig to 40 gig to 100 gig.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:17.440
By 2020, lot of the
public cloud links

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.590
will be at 100 gig,
everything 100 gig.

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:25.000
I don't think the catch-up
of the security industry

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:29.740
to do a line rate deep packet
inspection, line rate IDS

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.260
and IPS, will catch
up to 100 gig.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:36.700
That means you need to buy much
bigger boxes, maybe more boxes

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.862
to do 100-gig line rate
deep packet inspection.

00:14:39.862 --> 00:14:40.820
It's not going to work.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.730
So you have to think through
a completely different model

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:43.896
than removing these proxies.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.110
So with that, I start
off with clearly

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:52.300
introducing four new features.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:53.800
This is a slide you
should remember.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.840
I think takeaway
slide is, we are

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:59.140
proud to offer distributor
firewalls, enhancement

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:01.660
additional firewalls
or egress firewalls.

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:02.900
It's in alpha right now.

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:05.440
We'll go to beta pretty soon.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.980
The second feature is
VPC network sharing.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.680
Yesterday there was a
great talk about this.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:16.030
We are proud to announce a
beta of this two days ago.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.830
Third feature is VPC peering.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.090
It's a simple private peering.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:25.960
It's great for SaaS
providers, so consumers

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:30.770
can access all the
producer's SaaS services.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:33.550
The fourth feature is
super important for all

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.100
the third party appliances
to come to Google

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:39.310
and provide their virtual
appliances within Google

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:42.220
is multi-NIC support
for virtual machines.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:43.390
It is alpha right now.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:45.980
We're going to go
to beta pretty soon.

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:48.149
And there's one more
stuff in security, which I

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:49.440
don't want to talk about here--

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.231
[INAUDIBLE] gave the
presentation in this--

00:15:51.231 --> 00:15:53.080
is we have more
application-level security

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.260
coming up called
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:15:56.260 --> 00:16:00.190
We have custom IAM roles, and
we have data loss prevention.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:02.540
There's a nice demo
in the keynote.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:05.680
And then we are working more
towards content aware security

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:06.730
too.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:08.280
So you're going to see that.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:12.980
Let's focus on VPC alone in
this particular presentation.

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:17.860
Let's start with
a VPC topology 1.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.840
I have five topologies
or solutions

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:23.000
so you can think about how
you can use these solutions.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.490
Let's have an organization.

00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:27.320
Think example.com
or spotify.com.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:31.540
Then you wanted
the networks to be

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:33.970
isolated for a specific reason.

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:36.595
Maybe you bought a company or
there is a completely different

00:16:36.595 --> 00:16:37.095
formula.

00:16:37.095 --> 00:16:39.460
Let's say that engineering
department should not

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:43.330
want to see the quarterly
results of the sales

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:44.170
department.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:47.050
So you want to keep the sales
department completely isolated

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:48.310
in a different network.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:52.390
Even by mistake of the
firewall rules and policies,

00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:55.600
no engineer should be able to
watch our sales department's

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.025
quarterly reports.

00:16:57.025 --> 00:16:58.820
It's pure isolation.

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:00.010
This is one model.

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.055
So you have two
different networks

00:17:02.055 --> 00:17:04.180
under the same organization,
two different security

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:05.640
admins who do support it.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.109
And both networks are
global, by the way.

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:12.280
So Google's VPCs are global,
but they're isolated.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:16.050
You can bring up up to
five networks in a project.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:17.890
And within an organization,
you can bring up

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:20.349
hundreds of networks.

00:17:20.349 --> 00:17:24.040
There is absolutely-- we don't
limit it in an organization.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.124
If you have a project,
we'll limit to five.

00:17:26.124 --> 00:17:27.790
But if you call the
support, we can even

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:28.789
increase that limit too.

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:33.800
Now, if you have that,
now you can say, hey,

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:35.900
still I wanted some specific.

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:37.430
Let's say, my
[? NGVP ?] should be

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:43.547
able to access the sales one, or
my engineering development team

00:17:43.547 --> 00:17:45.380
should be able to access
the test department

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:46.430
or something like that.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:49.340
Then you can punch the hole
using specific public IPs.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.650
You can create them,
called forwarding IPs.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:53.660
And you can actually
create firewall rules.

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.930
Only those particular IPs are
going to talk to each other.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:59.480
And you can create firewall
rules in both networks.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.370
That means two
different networks are

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:03.710
going to talk on public IPs.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:05.850
And there is a second
way to do this,

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:08.662
is maybe because you might
think they're all public IPs,

00:18:08.662 --> 00:18:11.120
still there's a perception that
it's going to the internet.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:12.828
Actually, it's not
going to the internet.

00:18:12.828 --> 00:18:15.530
It is going through still
the Google's backbone

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:20.830
network, because those IPs
are still Google public IPs.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:22.960
Just to make the
things comfortable,

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:26.000
you can create VPN tunnels
between the two networks.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:27.800
It costs a little
bit more, because you

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:31.130
have to bring up this virtual
IPSec VPN gateways that Google

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.680
offers and cloud router
that Google offers,

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:35.180
but it just gives
you the security

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:39.380
that everything crossing the
two networks across the regions

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:41.400
is more secure in IPSec tunnels.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.740
Optimal topology number 3.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:48.940
This we want to be--
we're very proud of.

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:50.740
We started from scratch.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:54.650
This is called VPC
sharing, where you have,

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.210
as [INAUDIBLE] mentioned
yesterday, a lot of people

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:59.130
are trying to use Project.

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.515
I just want the project
to be by department

00:19:01.515 --> 00:19:03.180
where they have a
complete freedom

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:05.670
to create their compute
and storage resources.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.149
Networking is
completely different.

00:19:07.149 --> 00:19:08.690
I want to have a
different department

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.350
to handle the networking stuff.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.410
Let's create a global network
for all the departments

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:14.600
to attach to.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:16.550
And my security team
and networking team

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:19.950
is to make sure that no one
stepped onto each other.

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.320
That's the request we got
from all the customers.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.470
Hey, give us one network
where all my departments

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:26.934
can use that network.

00:19:26.934 --> 00:19:29.100
And we know how to create
firewall rules in that one

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:29.850
network.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.800
So we offer shared VPC.

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:38.460
Now, optimal topology number 4--

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.300
the same organization,
multi-NIC support.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:43.330
This is fantastic.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:45.850
This is a whole ecosystem
of third parties.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:47.410
We are working with
so many partners.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:50.490
You will see a talk and demo
from Palo Alto Networks.

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:53.250
If you have an organization,
again, same organization,

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.660
you have two networks.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:59.810
You want to make sure that
all the VMs in one network

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:02.060
has to funnel
through a set of VMs

00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:05.000
where you want to do all
kinds of security policies,

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.530
deep packet inspections, IDS,
IPS to be done in those VMs,

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:11.870
then use multi-NIC feature.

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:14.570
You can say that all
the consumer network has

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:18.070
to have a route to these
multi-NIC VMs in the producer

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.000
network, then you
can do, actually,

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:21.920
all the processing there.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:26.750
It's a kind of proxying, but
you have this option available.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:29.480
If you have services with all
the third party appliances,

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.790
you can tell them that please
bring it to Google Cloud.

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.010
They're happy to do it.

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:35.680
Optimal topology number 5--

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.340
VPC network peering.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.520
We are very proud to offer
this as an alpha thing.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.920
This is a game-changer, too.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:47.650
This is going to
provide a value-based

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.550
versus the cost-based for
all the SaaS providers.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:53.550
What that means is I am a
producer of some service

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.760
X, a SaaS service.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:20:58.620
If a customer or consumer
comes and asks me,

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.050
hey, I wanted your service,
let's say, Spanner service

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.430
or anything you wanted.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:04.410
I can bring up 10 [? to ?] VMs.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.320
I can charge 10 VMs' worth.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:10.510
They don't need to know how
many VMs I'm running for them.

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.900
It's completely hidden.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:14.790
Because two different
organizations,

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.650
two different
networks are peering,

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:20.860
all you need to do is
having a peering agreement.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.005
And we beautifully did
this in the UI itself.

00:21:24.005 --> 00:21:26.130
Two different organizations,
two different networks

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:27.990
can peer with each other.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:29.880
First, consumer or
producer has to say,

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.980
hey, I want to peer
with this organization,

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:36.120
this project, this network, and
then the call comes to that.

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.460
The other organization admin
has to accept in the UI,

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:40.170
saying that, OK, I
accept that I peer

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:42.430
with this other
organization network.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:43.260
Boom.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:45.030
Nothing, no proxies, nothing.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.250
Behind the scenes,
everything is a magic.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.450
We mesh all the VMs, full
mesh between the producer

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:51.600
and the consumer.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:52.740
There is no proxy.

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:56.377
The performance is
exactly the same.

00:21:56.377 --> 00:21:58.460
And whenever you want, you
can delete the peering.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:21:59.668
It's a bidirectional peering.

00:21:59.668 --> 00:22:02.180
Even a singular, say, a
producer decided that, like, I

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:03.830
don't want peering
with you anymore,

00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:04.960
you can cut the peering.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.455
All the routing will go away.

00:22:06.455 --> 00:22:08.330
It's a magic of routing,
distributed routing,

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.024
between the two networks.

00:22:10.024 --> 00:22:11.440
And once a peering
is established,

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:13.360
you can use the
actual load balancers.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.220
A consumer can actually talk
to the internal load balancing

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:17.900
[? VIP ?] inside
the producer network

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:21.480
and get the services done.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:22.480
And this is in all five.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:24.335
If you're interested,
please talk to us.

00:22:24.335 --> 00:22:25.210
We can whitelist you.

00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:30.000
The fourth feature in today--

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.260
there's a lot of features.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:34.030
Fourth new feature, we
are very happy to offer--

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:35.360
egress firewalls.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.640
So far, for a couple
of years, we offered--

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:39.150
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:42.520
--distributor firewalls--
thank you very much.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:48.070
Distributor firewalls, VM
to VM and internet to VM,

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:49.510
ingress side.

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:53.410
Exact same thing we
offer VM to internet.

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:56.060
Same connection tracking
firewall, stateful.

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:57.520
Everything is
stateful firewalls.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.230
We'll do connection tracking.

00:22:59.230 --> 00:23:03.850
We'll do priority, deny and
permit in all directions.

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:04.990
No choke points.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.900
Everything is distributor
routing, distributor firewalls.

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:17.850
Just to complete the
picture, GKE firewalls.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:21.310
Another big request for the
[? GCE ?] firewalls is, hey,

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:25.790
I have only limited quota, 200
firewall rules I can configure.

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:28.360
Hey, Subbaiah, can you increase
the quota for more firewall

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:28.859
rules?

00:23:28.859 --> 00:23:30.720
I have microsegmentation
going on.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.030
We are working on it.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:34.060
But if you're running
in containers,

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:35.330
you have another option.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:37.870
The rich ecosystem of,
actually, open source community

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:42.090
there in GKE, you can go
and create a GKE policy.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:43.630
It'll create a lot
of firewall rules

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:45.490
in iptables in your hosts.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:46.270
You can use that.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.300
And you can use,
actually, pretty well.

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.700
All the microsegmentation,
whatever you have in GKE,

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:58.380
you can use that to create,
behind the scenes iptables.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:02.640
And we are working on
all of these things.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:06.490
Another feature called private
access to Google Services.

00:24:06.490 --> 00:24:09.590
As you see, machine learning
services, analytics services,

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.480
storage services,
so many services

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.820
we offer in a
different namespace,

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:17.120
like highly available
things than from the VMs.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:21.290
But if we have a VPC with a
private IP address, RFC 1918 IP

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:25.250
space, you can access,
privately, those

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:28.730
services without having
any public IP address.

00:24:28.730 --> 00:24:31.100
You can just go to
the subnetwork's UI,

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:34.850
click, and say, Enable,
power private access

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:36.030
to Google services-- boom.

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:36.530
That's it.

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:37.280
Done.

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:38.710
Just one click away.

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:42.920
Yesterday there was a demo
of this in one of the talks.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:47.390
But that means any client from
the internet cannot access

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:49.280
their private VMs.

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:51.410
But if they want to access
the Google Services,

00:24:51.410 --> 00:24:53.452
they have to go through
these Google APIs.

00:24:53.452 --> 00:24:55.910
They need to have permission
to go through the Google APIs.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:01.250
More of the security stuff.

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:05.870
It's all documented in our
GCP nicely in our web pages.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.480
If you have VMs with
private IP addresses

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:10.550
and with our private--
with external addresses

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:11.966
and without external
addresses, we

00:25:11.966 --> 00:25:13.550
have a couple of
security things.

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:16.850
We do offer Bastion host
support, all GCloud commands.

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:20.319
We have SOCKS proxying
for SSH port tunneling.

00:25:20.319 --> 00:25:22.110
We have port forwarding
with SSH tunneling.

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:25.470
We have HTTPS with SSL
keys and without IP

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:26.420
external IP addresses.

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:27.620
We have NAT.

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:29.860
You can actually easily set
up your own NAT gateways.

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:36.200
All of these things,
four new features

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:39.440
we are offering in
networking, is available,

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:46.220
because of our cloud
networking distributor stack.

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:47.990
Basically, it's a
software-defined stack.

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:49.820
We call it Andromeda stack.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:52.474
And there is a talk
happened yesterday.

00:25:52.474 --> 00:25:53.640
You can watch it on YouTube.

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:56.630
We talked more about this.

00:25:56.630 --> 00:26:01.190
To recap, just remember,
so we have these features

00:26:01.190 --> 00:26:02.120
available for you--

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:08.200
egress firewalls, VPC sharing,
VPC peering, multi-NIC support.

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:10.320
With that, thank you very much.

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:13.841
Give it to Jigar from
Palo Alto Networks.

00:26:13.841 --> 00:26:14.590
Show you the demo.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:20.990
JIGAR SHAH: OK,
thank you, Subbaiah.

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:22.267
So my name is Jigar Shah.

00:26:22.267 --> 00:26:24.100
I'm a product manager
at Palo Alto Networks.

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:25.469
I focus on public clouds.

00:26:25.469 --> 00:26:27.260
So before I begin, I
would like to call out

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.320
that we're going to
show you a demo that

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:32.470
shows how we're going to provide
security in the Google Cloud

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:33.820
environment.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.530
So there's a few set
of new features coming.

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:38.350
I'm not going to talk
about all of them,

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:41.860
just the specific things that
I'm going to show in this demo.

00:26:41.860 --> 00:26:44.330
So before I go ahead,
a quick show of hands.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:48.060
How many of you are network
admins or networking experts?

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:49.160
OK, fair number.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:51.150
How about security guys?

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:51.790
OK.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:54.600
I see some of them, the same
hands, which just makes sense.

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:58.460
And how about application or
other infrastructure folks?

00:26:58.460 --> 00:26:58.960
OK.

00:26:58.960 --> 00:26:59.600
So a good mix.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:02.560
So this is sort of the
DevOps kind of world.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:05.740
So public cloud operates
in a shared security model,

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:08.020
right? [? Urs ?] mentioned
a whole bunch of security

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:09.260
features in the morning.

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:11.380
Subbaiah mentioned a
lot of others, right?

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:14.290
So Google takes care of
security off the cloud.

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:16.780
But once a packet hits
your VPC or your network,

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:19.660
it's your responsibility to
protect that environment.

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:21.910
And there's a lot of great
tools they give you, right?

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:24.170
IAM rules, Google
Cloud firewall,

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:26.290
ACL, all those components.

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:28.960
But even beyond
that, once the packet

00:27:28.960 --> 00:27:31.690
comes in, if there's
malware, there's no malware,

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:33.040
how do you look for that?

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.530
How do you protect against that?

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:40.687
So if you look at a Google Cloud
firewall, GCP firewall rules,

00:27:40.687 --> 00:27:42.020
they pretty much look like this.

00:27:42.020 --> 00:27:44.740
You can do controls at a five
[? topo ?] level-- source,

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:47.230
destination, port, protocol.

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:48.100
That's what you do.

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:50.395
So a typical transaction
would probably look like this.

00:27:50.395 --> 00:27:52.690
This is the information
you see in the wire.

00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:55.970
And it's a 344-kilobyte
transaction.

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:58.240
What if you could actually
look at more things

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.530
and make decisions
based on that?

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:03.040
So for example, oh,
it's not just 443,

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:06.580
it's actually HTTP
running on SSL.

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:09.110
And oh, this is
the specific user

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:13.270
and they belong to this
specific LDAP or AD user group.

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:17.190
And the destination
traffic is actually

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:21.760
geomapped to some user in Canada
or some destination in Canada.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:24.230
What are the
attributes of that URL?

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:26.860
Is it a known command
control server or is it

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:30.417
just regular commercial
proper, benign server?

00:28:30.417 --> 00:28:32.500
So there's all these other
additional intelligence

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:35.740
and metadata that can be
valuable in making a decision.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:38.680
And in addition, just
because it's HTTP

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:40.130
doesn't mean anything anymore.

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:43.640
Everything is pretty much HTTP,
especially in the public cloud.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:47.460
So, for example, if it's a
slide-sharing application, oh,

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:50.680
and it's got a payload off a PDF
file being transferred, right?

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:53.094
So those are important
attributes you want to look at.

00:28:53.094 --> 00:28:54.760
Now, this is a benign
example of a flow,

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:57.644
let's say, going out from
a Google Cloud VM outbound.

00:28:57.644 --> 00:28:59.060
Everything should
allow it, right?

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:01.360
It looks all perfect and benign.

00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:05.500
But your firewall rules,
if this was actually

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:07.870
some real malware, like
a pre-executable that's

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:09.910
going through and
it's got malware,

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:13.280
you wouldn't know how to
control for it, in effect.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:16.315
So if you look at an
attack lifecycle--

00:29:16.315 --> 00:29:18.110
and this is one
manifestation; this is

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.110
a famous framework, in effect--

00:29:20.110 --> 00:29:24.580
that typical exploit vectors
happen from either an insider

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.430
making some mistakes
or a malicious insider

00:29:27.430 --> 00:29:29.020
or an outsider doing a breach.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.480
So essentially, I like to
call it a north-south problem.

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:32.800
Somebody's breached in.

00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:36.020
And then they want to branch off
and find out where next to go,

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:38.240
whether it's on-prem
or in the cloud.

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.060
And a lot of use cases
are hybrid, right?

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:42.895
You may have a big application
in the public cloud,

00:29:42.895 --> 00:29:44.770
but there might be some
connection going back

00:29:44.770 --> 00:29:45.850
to on-prem.

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:49.012
That becomes a really important
vector for the attack.

00:29:49.012 --> 00:29:51.220
And the most common things
we see in terms of attacks

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:53.110
is, you know, people
want to steal your data.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.651
That's the most
valuable asset you've

00:29:54.651 --> 00:29:57.910
got, whether it's user
information, credit card

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:00.400
information, so on.

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:04.210
Now we are also starting to
see botnets also starting up.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:06.500
If you look at the
data out there,

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:09.280
a number of public VMs,
when they get hijacked,

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:12.880
because mostly of poor
security practices,

00:30:12.880 --> 00:30:14.410
they become really good botnets.

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:17.050
Because the bandwidth
available from a public cloud

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:20.440
environment's
enormously well capable.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:21.850
It's very powerful networking.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:26.140
So botnets have a very easy
way of spreading around.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:28.090
And then less
often, we also start

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:30.760
to see that you've got really
good compute resources.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:33.367
Let's run a bitcoin
mining operation.

00:30:33.367 --> 00:30:34.450
It's really cheap compute.

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.970
As long as you get it,
it's free money, right?

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:40.660
So to prevent this, these
type of attacks, what you need

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.240
is complete visibility in
the enterprise network.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:45.930
And in the cloud, in the
mobile, you need visibility.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:48.460
What you can't see, you
can't really protect.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.921
The next element is
reducing the attack surface.

00:30:50.921 --> 00:30:52.420
So you're obviously
trying to narrow

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:55.480
that if it's a web location,
you may set up GCP firewall

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:58.030
rules to say open 40 and 443.

00:30:58.030 --> 00:31:01.125
You do all the basic things
to say reduce attack surface.

00:31:01.125 --> 00:31:02.500
If you know the
applications, you

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:05.500
can identify applications, put
controls based on applications

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:06.730
specifically.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:10.560
And then comes the more
intelligent part to say,

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:11.740
is there actually malware?

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.080
Are these all known
CV and vulnerabilities

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:15.540
that are out there known?

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:17.710
Let's block them
right out of the box.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:20.440
So the attacker should not
have an opportunity to say,

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:21.594
I'm going to go hack this.

00:31:21.594 --> 00:31:24.010
Obviously, this requires that
all your systems are patched

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:27.190
correctly as often as
possible, so you're protected

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:29.010
against known vulnerabilities.

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:31.240
But what about unknown
vulnerabilities, right?

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:33.730
So these can be zero day attacks
or brand new attacks that

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:37.690
are not zero day caliber, but
people don't know about them.

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.000
How quickly can we
fix them, right?

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.060
Because the goal is to do
prevention and not really say,

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:44.080
OK, we found a problem.

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:44.970
Let's go fix it.

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:46.650
Throw in the incident
response team.

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:49.540
Spend lots of hours and
dollars on consulting people

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:51.640
coming doing IR and forensics.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:54.250
You want to put up as much
protection as possible,

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:57.100
so you're not spending
money later on.

00:31:57.100 --> 00:32:00.817
So to do this requires a lot
of capabilities under the hood.

00:32:00.817 --> 00:32:02.650
So I'm not going to go
through these things,

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:05.290
because there's a lot
of detail in there.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:07.810
But I'll pick a quick
specific example.

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:10.180
Let's say there's a
malware that is spreading.

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:13.040
It's a known malware.

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:14.440
What application
is it attacking?

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:16.390
Typically, a malware
is looking for exploits

00:32:16.390 --> 00:32:19.060
in specific applications
or specific binaries.

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:24.520
Often that application
is going to be encrypted.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:27.080
Pretty much 70% or
more of applications

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:28.312
are getting encrypted.

00:32:28.312 --> 00:32:29.770
So you want a
solution that's going

00:32:29.770 --> 00:32:33.500
to be able to even do SSL
decrypt when it's necessary.

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:36.210
And then you want
to have coverage,

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:37.710
like I was saying,
across the board.

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:40.910
So obviously, cloud visibility
is really important.

00:32:40.910 --> 00:32:43.750
And then for those unknown
things, like I said,

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:46.200
there's a lot of machine
learning, dynamic analysis,

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:47.600
static analysis techniques.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:48.850
So I'm not going into details.

00:32:48.850 --> 00:32:50.770
But those are
typical capabilities

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:54.010
you'd need to sort of
provide that protection.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:56.440
So let me switch
gears and say how

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:59.320
does this manifest in the
Google Cloud environment?

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:02.800
So the idea is that to bring a
next generation firewall, which

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:05.350
has all these capabilities.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:06.740
We needed a
multi-NIC capability.

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:08.680
So Google has that
capability, so that you

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:11.089
can create clean segmentation.

00:33:11.089 --> 00:33:12.630
So you have their
management network,

00:33:12.630 --> 00:33:14.004
you may have a
public-facing side

00:33:14.004 --> 00:33:17.290
network for internet-based
focused services,

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:19.570
and then you may have
multiple private networks,

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:21.340
where you're running
your applications

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:22.540
in different tiers.

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:25.300
So the firewall can now
connect across all of them

00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:28.210
and provide that north-south
and east-west security

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:29.835
between application tiers.

00:33:29.835 --> 00:33:31.590
Obviously, this is
a single firewall.

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:34.390
I wouldn't recommend anybody
to put this up right now,

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:36.340
because it's a single
point of failure.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:38.950
What you need is, obviously,
a scale-out architecture,

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:40.760
that you have load
balancers that

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:42.430
are front-ending your service.

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:44.500
You put security behind it.

00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:47.080
And then you put your
application tier behind it.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:50.020
Now, obviously, this is a
static picture, so to speak,

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:52.030
and the cloud is all
about elasticity.

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:54.370
So as demand for
your services grow,

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:56.694
your web tier on your
front end tier is growing,

00:33:56.694 --> 00:33:58.360
other things behind
it are automatically

00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:01.600
scaling, the firewall and
the security services also

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:03.040
need to scale behind it.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.230
And this has to be
very seamless to you,

00:34:05.230 --> 00:34:07.790
which means you need
bootstrapping and automation.

00:34:07.790 --> 00:34:09.250
But it has to be transparent.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:11.560
You can't be spending time
to say, oh, stuff scaled up,

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:14.739
I need to do something.

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:18.969
So I showed internet-facing
scale-out architecture.

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:22.060
There's obviously other use
cases, primarily, hybrid cloud,

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:25.630
meaning on-prem connecting into
the Google Cloud environment.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:27.489
There's very specific
security scenarios

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:29.104
that need to be covered there.

00:34:29.104 --> 00:34:30.520
There's segmentation,
like I said,

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:32.770
between application tiers.

00:34:32.770 --> 00:34:34.375
And then between
different projects,

00:34:34.375 --> 00:34:37.330
like Subbaiah was
showing, when you have,

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:40.620
let's say, department 1
talking to department 2,

00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:42.670
where department
1 or application 1

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:44.090
has a higher trust level.

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:45.790
The other one has a
lower trust level.

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:47.690
If you were going to
do full VPC peering,

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:49.719
do you really want
that full mesh?

00:34:49.719 --> 00:34:53.230
If it's between two networks
of equal trust, it makes sense.

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:55.329
If there are networks
of different trust,

00:34:55.329 --> 00:34:57.370
you probably want to do
deeper inspection to say,

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:59.320
if I have this
partner connecting in,

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:01.900
I want to let them access
to only these things.

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:04.470
I'm going to make sure
that if they are hacked,

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:06.730
I don't get hacked.

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:08.660
And then the
internet-facing scale

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:12.590
out perimeter
capability I showed

00:35:12.590 --> 00:35:14.140
with the scale-out story.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:15.850
So I'll give you
a demo, so that it

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:18.310
becomes a little bit
more apparent of what

00:35:18.310 --> 00:35:20.034
we're bringing to the table.

00:35:20.034 --> 00:35:21.700
So I'm going to switch
over to the demo.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:31.080
So here you see typical
Google Cloud networks set up

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:32.190
in a project.

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:33.440
I've got four networks set up.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:34.814
So I've got the
database network.

00:35:34.814 --> 00:35:37.840
I've got a management network
for the management traffic,

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.430
an internet-facing public trough
network, and a web network.

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:48.430
And on the Compute side,
I've got three VMs only,

00:35:48.430 --> 00:35:50.200
very simple demo in that sense.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:53.180
So this is the firewall, the
next generation firewall.

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:56.470
It's a four NIC setup, like
I showed in the diagram.

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:59.920
And there's a database
network and a web server

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:02.350
network where the database
and the web server are living.

00:36:02.350 --> 00:36:04.120
The web server's
running a simple Apache

00:36:04.120 --> 00:36:05.950
server and a WordPress server.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:07.450
And the WordPress
server has content

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.200
that's living in the database.

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:12.265
So the demo's intention
is to sort of show you

00:36:12.265 --> 00:36:15.825
that north-south and that
east-west protection.

00:36:15.825 --> 00:36:18.570
So this is my web server.

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:19.480
This is the Apache.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:20.320
It's really simple.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:21.700
I load it up.

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:23.620
And it's got the
typical content.

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:26.530
And as usual, obligatory,
you got to put a cat video.

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:31.060
So there's HTTP
content coming in.

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:34.149
So if I go ahead and
look into the firewall,

00:36:34.149 --> 00:36:35.190
I mean, it's kind of fun.

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:37.810
You put up, let's say, a
good monitoring solution,

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:40.360
like us, that's just an
application visibility service,

00:36:40.360 --> 00:36:43.000
and you should see the number
of people doing port scans.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:44.580
And then once you
see a open port,

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:47.050
they start trying to
poke through the holes.

00:36:47.050 --> 00:36:48.895
So this is the interface
for the firewall.

00:36:52.090 --> 00:36:55.140
So let me first walk
you through the policies

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:56.735
I've configured in this demo.

00:36:56.735 --> 00:36:59.110
So the first thing you see is
the left side is the source

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:01.240
side and the right side
is the destination.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:04.900
So I see Inbound Web going
from the public internet side

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:06.280
to the web tier.

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:08.460
And I said Allow Web Browsing.

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:11.010
So I'm not saying allow anything
else, just web browsing.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:12.760
We're going to look
at the packets to say,

00:37:12.760 --> 00:37:16.390
are these actual HTTP packets,
HTTP transaction, right?

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:17.890
Nothing else should be allowed.

00:37:17.890 --> 00:37:23.060
And then web to DB, web
area going to database.

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:25.960
So what I'm saying is Allow
Traffic Only, actually

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:29.170
specifically MySQL, because
by starting and identifying

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:31.670
the patterns, we know
what MySQL looks like.

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:34.270
So we're saying, OK,
allow MySQL only.

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:36.294
And the third thing
I'm doing is all

00:37:36.294 --> 00:37:38.710
the Linux VMs, the web server,
the database, they probably

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:39.970
need to do updates.

00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:42.160
So I'm going from
web and database

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:43.390
to the public internet.

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:46.890
And I'm saying only allow
updates for apt-get or yum

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:47.870
updates.

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:49.690
I could even further
lock it down and say,

00:37:49.690 --> 00:37:51.760
you can only go to Canonical
to get your updates

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:52.990
or whatever your repo is.

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:55.385
So you can do some very
specific lockdowns.

00:37:55.385 --> 00:37:57.010
And then I've got
the special exception

00:37:57.010 --> 00:38:00.460
to say allow SSH only for
my corporate IP address.

00:38:00.460 --> 00:38:03.116
And my corporate IP
addresses are loaded here.

00:38:03.116 --> 00:38:04.990
So that way I can do
specific management even

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:08.980
over the open internet, but
still locked down to my IPs.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:11.590
So if I go to the
Monitor tab, this

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:14.210
is showing the traffic
logs of our firewall.

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:18.040
So if I do a refresh,
you can see that there's

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:20.140
a lot of traffic coming in.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:22.290
OK, shouldn't have
hit Refresh there.

00:38:27.574 --> 00:38:29.490
You can see there's a
lot of traffic coming in

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:30.710
from the open internet.

00:38:30.710 --> 00:38:32.460
And you can see a lot
of people are trying

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:34.050
to connect to my public IP.

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:37.584
By the way, this public IP
has no real application.

00:38:37.584 --> 00:38:40.000
But somebody's sitting there
and just constantly scanning,

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:41.725
looking for the stuff.

00:38:41.725 --> 00:38:43.350
You can see, obviously,
there's traffic

00:38:43.350 --> 00:38:47.010
coming from [? Mosconi, ?]
which is the page I just loaded.

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:49.050
So that was the Apache server.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:51.180
And we obviously identified.

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:53.790
And sometimes we will
not get enough packets

00:38:53.790 --> 00:38:57.030
to say what kind of traffic it
is, so we're saying incomplete.

00:38:57.030 --> 00:39:00.590
But let's say, I go back
and reload the page.

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:03.220
And then just got
to refresh the--

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:05.450
oops.

00:39:05.450 --> 00:39:06.350
All right.

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:08.410
[INAUDIBLE] scroll across.

00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:11.410
Let me maximize
this, so it's easier.

00:39:11.410 --> 00:39:15.580
So if I refresh the
data, you're going

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:19.840
to see there's traffic for
web browsing coming in.

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:26.290
So now, similarly, if I have a
WordPress on that same server,

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:28.110
so I reload the WordPress pages.

00:39:28.110 --> 00:39:30.450
So this obviously is
going to go transaction

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:31.620
with the WordPress server.

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:33.880
That's going to reach
into the SQL database.

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:39.640
So if I go back and look at
the logs, what I'm seeing

00:39:39.640 --> 00:39:42.280
is traffic coming in from the
public internet to the web

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:47.440
zone, going to its
web browsing traffic.

00:39:47.440 --> 00:39:50.440
And then what you
would expect to see

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:52.435
is further down
is the web server

00:39:52.435 --> 00:39:54.790
is reaching into the
database and SQL transactions

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:55.889
are happening there.

00:39:55.889 --> 00:39:57.430
So I'm doing a very
specific lockdown

00:39:57.430 --> 00:39:59.800
to say these are the only
flows you should allow based

00:39:59.800 --> 00:40:01.540
on the application patterns.

00:40:01.540 --> 00:40:04.240
Now, in addition, you can
obviously enable deeper IDS/IDS

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:06.940
things, so that if your
web server's compromised

00:40:06.940 --> 00:40:09.520
and somebody's trying to do a
SQL injection or another attack

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:11.970
on the database, we're
able to provide that.

00:40:11.970 --> 00:40:13.720
Now, the more interesting
news, obviously,

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:15.580
is also the exfiltration part.

00:40:15.580 --> 00:40:18.730
So you can make sure that
somebody cannot do mass FTP

00:40:18.730 --> 00:40:23.560
transfers or mass HTTP
transfers or big file outflows.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:25.990
So those are the things that
we intend to sort of provide

00:40:25.990 --> 00:40:27.790
in terms of protection.

00:40:27.790 --> 00:40:30.940
So there's a demo at our
booth in the expo floor

00:40:30.940 --> 00:40:32.230
if you want to take a look.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:34.720
And there's more
information on the website.

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:36.160
So that's all I
had for the demo.

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:39.340
If you've got questions, I
can take them afterwards.

00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:44.090
So after that, I'll hand
it over to Prajakta.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:45.385
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:45.385 --> 00:40:47.010
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Which
one should I use?

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:47.551
Which laptop?

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:00.010
So hi, folks.

00:41:00.010 --> 00:41:01.120
I'm Prajakta.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:04.390
I'm a product manager
in Cloud Networking.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:06.430
So how many of you stay
up at night thinking

00:41:06.430 --> 00:41:07.540
about DDoS attacks?

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:10.740
Nobody?

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:12.480
Your networks are super secure.

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:13.414
Yeah.

00:41:13.414 --> 00:41:15.330
And I'm sure they're on
Google Cloud, which is

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:16.621
why you don't stay up at night.

00:41:16.621 --> 00:41:17.970
So that's great.

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:20.400
So most of you know
about DDoS attacks.

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:22.830
I'm not going to go into them.

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:24.780
Most of you know that
they're also classified

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:26.370
into three categories--

00:41:26.370 --> 00:41:29.160
network, connection,
application.

00:41:29.160 --> 00:41:31.710
Each of these attacks has
interesting characters.

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:34.710
So the layer 3 will try to
just overwhelm your network

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:37.140
and consume all the bandwidth.

00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:38.850
With layer 4, basically,
they're trying

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:42.210
to exhaust any type of
component in your network that

00:41:42.210 --> 00:41:44.130
has a state table.

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:46.830
And the layer 7 ones try to
exploit the characteristics

00:41:46.830 --> 00:41:49.910
of your application.

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:52.090
Now, here is the
interesting bit.

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:54.580
Most of you may
not know, but when

00:41:54.580 --> 00:41:56.950
you're actually on
Google Cloud Platform

00:41:56.950 --> 00:42:00.430
and you're using Google
Cloud load balancing or CDN,

00:42:00.430 --> 00:42:04.630
your deployments have
inbuilt or rather implicit

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:07.522
DDoS protection for
layer 3 and layer 4.

00:42:07.522 --> 00:42:10.450
And you can, of course,
complement this with offerings

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:14.954
from our other partners and
our third party providers.

00:42:14.954 --> 00:42:16.120
So let's take a closer look.

00:42:19.030 --> 00:42:21.380
You have your VM.

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:25.070
What may not be obvious is that
you actually have throttles

00:42:25.070 --> 00:42:29.577
per VM at 10 Gbps, which means
even if you get attacked,

00:42:29.577 --> 00:42:32.160
there is going to be no impact
to your standing as a customer.

00:42:34.830 --> 00:42:37.804
Next, if you actually deploy
Google Cloud load balancing--

00:42:37.804 --> 00:42:39.720
and you know that it
comes in various flavors,

00:42:39.720 --> 00:42:42.120
such as HTTP/HTTPS
load balancing,

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:45.360
SSL proxy, PCP proxy.

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:47.460
So if you implement
that, you do actually

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:50.040
get protection against
layer 3 and layer 4 attacks,

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:53.790
so attacks such as SYN floods,
IP fragment floods, and so on.

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:56.160
And in fact, HTTP/HTTPS
load balancing

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.500
not only protects you
against these attacks,

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:03.270
but other attacks, such
as Slowloris and so on.

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:06.490
And then, of course, you can
use Cloud CDN along with that.

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:08.220
So you can serve
your legitimate users

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:11.270
from the edge of our network.

00:43:11.270 --> 00:43:13.100
This one just recaps
what I spoke about.

00:43:13.100 --> 00:43:16.100
So I'm actually going
to skip this slide.

00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:19.050
So I wanted to tell you
an interesting story.

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:22.175
How many of you have
heard of Brian Krebs?

00:43:22.175 --> 00:43:23.860
Great.

00:43:23.860 --> 00:43:26.470
So for those of you who
did not raise your hand,

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:28.330
he's an investigative
journalist.

00:43:28.330 --> 00:43:30.820
He actually exposes criminals.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:34.735
He has a website out
called KrebsOnSecurity.com.

00:43:34.735 --> 00:43:37.960
As you can imagine,
when he did that,

00:43:37.960 --> 00:43:39.580
the criminals came after him.

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:41.970
They started
DDoSing his website.

00:43:41.970 --> 00:43:44.740
And the attacks
got so huge that he

00:43:44.740 --> 00:43:46.840
was asked by his previous
provider to move,

00:43:46.840 --> 00:43:50.380
because it would have impacted
their other customers.

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:52.360
He came to Google for help.

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:56.860
And so we hosted him for free
on Google Cloud infrastructure

00:43:56.860 --> 00:43:59.200
under a project we
call Project Shield.

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:02.380
So it is to protect
the freedom of speech

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:04.480
and also to protect
journalists and then

00:44:04.480 --> 00:44:09.295
other entities that participate
in elections and so on.

00:44:09.295 --> 00:44:12.080
Now, here is an interesting
set of events that were

00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:13.750
told to me by our DDoS expert.

00:44:13.750 --> 00:44:17.500
This is how it actually rolled
out after KrebsOnSecurity came

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:20.320
to the Google Cloud Platform.

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:25.870
So when he came back up, it was
hit with 130 million packets

00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:27.700
per second of SYN floods.

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:30.430
And for those of you who are
familiar with Mirai botnets,

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:32.650
this was precisely that.

00:44:32.650 --> 00:44:35.200
It also received additional
60 million packets

00:44:35.200 --> 00:44:37.400
per seconds of resets.

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:41.072
The attack was huge,
but there was no impact.

00:44:41.072 --> 00:44:43.030
And this is because the
SYN floods were handled

00:44:43.030 --> 00:44:45.910
by Google infrastructure.

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:48.460
Now, a minute later,
the attack shifted.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:51.430
It was 250 gig queries
per second attack

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:54.316
from about 145K Mirai IPs.

00:44:54.316 --> 00:44:56.230
And this is a good
example of how

00:44:56.230 --> 00:45:02.150
attackers try random stuff until
they find something that works.

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:04.820
An hour later, there
were a few more attacks,

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:07.400
but there was no
impact to his site.

00:45:07.400 --> 00:45:10.727
The Google infrastructure
continued to shield it.

00:45:10.727 --> 00:45:12.560
At this point, there
was a bit of a respite.

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:15.630
So the attacks
stopped for some time.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:17.370
Next morning, Brian was happy.

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:20.797
He posted this post, called "The
Democratization of Censorship"

00:45:20.797 --> 00:45:23.130
and how Google infrastructure
was hosting and protecting

00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:24.990
his website.

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:27.270
Four hours after
this blog post, he

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:32.130
was hit with 450-gig queries
per second of HTTP floods

00:45:32.130 --> 00:45:34.210
from 175K Mirai IPs.

00:45:37.570 --> 00:45:40.540
And this went on and on and on.

00:45:40.540 --> 00:45:42.520
There were dozens of attacks.

00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:45.490
But it also gave our team
a really great opportunity

00:45:45.490 --> 00:45:49.120
to validate Google's defenses.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:51.760
So why am I telling
you this story?

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:54.215
First, the happy ending.

00:45:54.215 --> 00:45:59.470
KrebsOnSecurity.com continues to
run unimpacted on Google Cloud.

00:45:59.470 --> 00:46:01.480
But I told you
about this incident,

00:46:01.480 --> 00:46:04.960
because this same infrastructure
protects your deployments

00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:08.000
in the Google Cloud.

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:10.250
This happens when you enable
either Google Cloud load

00:46:10.250 --> 00:46:14.510
balancing or Google Cloud
load balancing with Cloud CDN.

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:16.190
And you can
definitely complement

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:20.360
this with services and appliance
from our third party providers.

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:21.950
And so to talk more
about that, I'd

00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:26.850
like to invite Michelle, the
co-founder of Cloudflare.

